module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FreeModule.Finite.Basic

/-!
# Gordan's lemma for lattice points in a rational cone

For a free abelian group `L` of finite rank, regarded in `L ⊗[ℤ] ℚ` through `x ↦ x ⊗ 1`, and
vectors `v₁, …, v_r ∈ L` (`r = 0` allowed), the lattice points of the rational cone spanned by
the `vᵢ` form a finite union of translates of the monoid of natural combinations of the `vᵢ`,
and hence a finitely generated commutative monoid.

## Main statements

* `gordan_rational_cone_finite`: the finite set `F`, the translate decomposition and the
  generation of `C ∩ L` by `F` and the `vᵢ`.

Source: literature/illusie-laszlo-orgogozo-travaux-de-gabber_arXiv-1207.3648.pdf, Exposé VIII,
3.2.9, proof, printed p. 104 (finite toric saturation)
(blueprint: lem-trf-gordan-rational-cone-finite).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

/--
Gordan's lemma for the lattice points in a rational cone.  Let `L` be free of finite rank over
`ℤ`, regarded in `L ⊗[ℤ] ℚ`, let `v : Fin r → L`, let `C` be the set of nonnegative rational
combinations of the `vᵢ` and `P` the set of their natural combinations.  Then there is a finite
set `F ⊆ C ∩ L` with `C ∩ L = ⋃_{f ∈ F} (f + P)`, and `C ∩ L` is the additive monoid generated by
`F` and the `vᵢ`.

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Exposé VIII, 3.2.9, proof, printed p. 104 (finite toric
saturation); the elementary fundamental-parallelepiped argument below is written by the agent.

Proof (no node is used; every step is elementary).
1. Write `vᵢ = ∑ⱼ aᵢⱼ eⱼ` in a basis `e₁, …, e_d` of `L`.  Let
   `Q = {∑ᵢ tᵢ vᵢ : tᵢ ∈ ℚ, 0 ≤ tᵢ < 1}` and `Bⱼ = ∑ᵢ |aᵢⱼ|`.  The `j`-th coordinate of a point
   of `Q` has absolute value at most `Bⱼ`, so the coordinate map injects `Q ∩ L` into the finite
   product `∏ⱼ ([-Bⱼ, Bⱼ] ∩ ℤ)`.  Put `F = Q ∩ L`; it is finite and lies in `C ∩ L`.  Empty sums
   and products give `F = {0}` when `d = 0` or `r = 0`.
2. For `x = ∑ᵢ qᵢ vᵢ ∈ C ∩ L` with `qᵢ ≥ 0`, put `nᵢ = ⌊qᵢ⌋ ∈ ℕ` and `tᵢ = qᵢ - nᵢ ∈ [0, 1)`.
   Then `p = ∑ᵢ nᵢ vᵢ ∈ P` and `f = ∑ᵢ tᵢ vᵢ = x - p` lies in `Q` and in `L`, so `f ∈ F` and
   `x = f + p`.  Conversely, for `f ∈ F` and `p ∈ P`, adding the nonnegative rational
   coefficients gives `f + p ∈ C`, and `f + p ∈ L`.  Hence `C ∩ L = ⋃_{f ∈ F} (f + P)`.
3. By step 2 every element of `C ∩ L` is an element of `F` plus a natural combination of the
   `vᵢ`, so it lies in the closure of `F ∪ {vᵢ}`; conversely `C ∩ L` is closed under addition,
   contains `0`, each `f ∈ F` and each `vᵢ`.  So `C ∩ L` is the monoid generated by `F` and the
   `vᵢ`.
(blueprint: lem-trf-gordan-rational-cone-finite)
-/
theorem gordan_rational_cone_finite
    (L : Type u) [AddCommGroup L] [Module ℤ L] [Module.Free ℤ L] [Module.Finite ℤ L]
    (r : ℕ) (v : Fin r → L) :
    let ι : L → TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ :=
      fun x => TensorProduct.mk ℤ L ℚ x 1
    let C : Set (TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ) :=
      {x | ∃ q : Fin r → ℚ, (∀ i, 0 ≤ q i) ∧ x = ∑ i, TensorProduct.mk ℤ L ℚ (v i) (q i)}
    let P : Set (TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ) :=
      {x | ∃ n : Fin r → ℕ, x = ∑ i, TensorProduct.mk ℤ L ℚ (v i) (n i : ℚ)}
    let Lset : Set (TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ) := Set.range ι
    ∃ F : Finset (TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ),
      (∀ f ∈ F, f ∈ C ∩ Lset) ∧
        C ∩ Lset = ⋃ f ∈ (F : Set (TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ)),
          {x | ∃ p ∈ P, x = f + p} ∧
        AddSubmonoid.closure ((F : Set (TensorProduct ℤ L ℚ)) ∪
            Set.range (fun i => ι (v i))) = C ∩ Lset := by
  sorry
